# Conventions — {{CAMPAIGN_NAME}} PM

The operating law for this manager repo. Fixed sections are framework law (do not weaken);
the config block at the bottom is this campaign's specifics.

## 1. Numbering

- **Issues** — `I-###`, one global sequence across all repos, each tagged with its repo(s):
  `I-005 [{{PRIMARY_REPO}}]`. States: OPEN, ACTIVE (a current front), IN-PROGRESS
  (dispatched), TODO (queued), PARKED, BLOCKED (on another ticket), NEEDS-USER (awaiting a
  user call), CLOSED.
- **Questions to agents** — `Q-###`; the recorded answer takes the same number as `A-###`.
- **Reports** — `R-###`, plus a version and a date (§2).
- **Dispatches** — `D-###`, immutable per dispatch; attempts within a dispatch are `A-##`
  (§4). The dispatch id is the join key threading an issue → prompt file → worker tab →
  branch → result.

## 2. Reports: numbered, versioned, dated

Filename: `reports/R-###_v#_YYYY-MM-DD_slug.md`. The `R-###` identifies the document; a
revision keeps the number, bumps `v#`, carries the new date. Every report opens with a
one-line TL;DR and grounds every claim in ticket numbers, versions, or commit SHAs.

## 3. State is the EVENT LOG — Markdown is narrative

`.pm/events.log` (grammar: `bin/` reads it; humans don't) is the **single source of truth
for state**. The Markdown files below are a human narrative and are **never parsed for
state** — edit them freely. `.pm/index.json` is a regenerable fold of the log; delete and
`bin/reconcile` rebuilds it. Rule on any disagreement: **the event log + live git/herdr win;
prose is re-rendered, never trusted as state.** Generated summary blocks sit inside
`<!-- GENERATED:BEGIN key -->…<!-- END -->` markers — anything you hand-type inside a marker
is lost on the next `reconcile`.

`STRATEGY.md` is narrative like the rest — never parsed for state — but jointly owned: the
manager proposes strategy updates (milestones, workplan) and confirms them with the user
before writing, at milestone boundaries or when the path changes, never unilaterally. Keep it
short.

## 4. Dispatch identity, lanes, and the result contract

- **Two lanes, always available; you direct which handles a dispatch:**
  - *Automation lane* — a script spawns a herdr worker tab pre-loaded with the prompt and
    collects the result. Requires herdr machine-readable output.
  - *Human-gated lane* — you spawn the tab / paste the prompt / relay the result.
- **Lane ownership is recorded at dispatch** (`READY→DISPATCHED` writes `lane`, `prompt_sha`,
  attempt; `tab` resolves at ACK). A dispatch owned by one lane is refused by the other.
- **Out-of-protocol work is absorbed, not hidden.** Work that appears with no matching
  dispatch is logged `unregistered_execution`; you `adopt` it into a D. The system detects
  bypass; it does not pretend to prevent it.
- **Always-human gates (both lanes):** merges to shared/mainline branches, pushes/uploads,
  and any `NEEDS-USER` item NEVER auto-execute.
- **Verifier contract** — every issue declares, at grill/issue creation and WITH the user:
  its **Verifier** (the concrete command, check, or artifact that proves it's done) and its
  **Approval gates** (the actions requiring explicit user approval). Both live on the
  `LEDGER.md` issue, are carried — grounded per §8, never as a ticket number — into every
  dispatch prompt for that issue, are answered by the worker's Verification section, and are
  what the human VERIFY step checks: verification means running/inspecting the pre-agreed
  verifier, not re-deriving from scratch what would prove the work.
- **Result contract (verification-first)** — every dispatched worker structures its return
  as three sections: **Completed** (what was done: touched files, commit SHAs/branches,
  did-it-change-product-code) · **Verification** (the declared verifier from the prompt,
  run/produced, with its outcome — PASS/FAIL; "could not run it" is said plainly, never
  papered over) · **Remaining Work** (anything undone, blocked, escalated, or
  user-action-required), plus the dispatch id as the report-back label. This is a
  disciplined intake + audit trail, corroborated by `reconcile`'s independent git read —
  NOT a security guarantee.
- **`bin/track --once`** — a manager-triggered stateless reconciliation tick. It takes one
  fresh `herdr api snapshot`, and for every `ACKED` dispatch whose recorded tab now shows
  `done`, checks the worker's branch tip against git: fully corroborated cases are
  auto-recorded `RETURNED` (intake only, never auto-verified); everything else — ambiguous
  history, a missing/reused tab, no commit — is surfaced in `TRACKER.md` for you to work by
  hand. Verification and close stay human-gated regardless of what `track` records — except
  the opt-in auto-close pass (`automation.auto_close`, default OFF), which closes an issue
  only after human VERIFY plus strict git-topology corroboration. Squash/rebase repos
  (`merge_mode: "squash"`) may additionally close on a human-attested `record merged`
  marker when strict topology cannot see the squash. A marker close is human attestation
  sanity-checked by git (the attested merge commit must be real and on mainline), NOT git
  corroboration that the work itself landed — opting a repo into `merge_mode: "squash"`
  means "I accept human-attested auto-closes for this repo". Such closes say
  `auto-closed-marker` (or `auto-closed-marker-branch-deleted`, which also needs the
  per-repo `allow_marker_branch_deleted: true` opt-in) instead of `auto-closed-strict`,
  and each one leaves a durable `auto-close:*` audit note on the event log.
- **Automation-lane auto-spawn (B3, opt-in `automation.auto_spawn`, default OFF).** The
  human still authors the prompt and prepares the dispatch —
  `bin/dispatch-prep --lane automation` mints `lane=automation tab=?`, COPIES the prompt to
  the durable `prompts/I-###_<slug>_YYYY-MM-DD.md` path, and emits a
  `note d=<D> ref=prompts/...` binding; prep never spawns. Each `bin/track --once` tick
  then works the queue (automation-lane `DISPATCHED` dispatches with `tab=?`, FIFO by
  dispatch number, excluding NEEDS-USER/BLOCKED issues, issues with open questions,
  superseded dispatches, and dispatches with an open intent) under a **durable
  spawn-intent lease**: per free capacity slot (live agents in the configured workspace vs
  `automation.max_live_workers`), track re-resolves the prompt from the folded note
  binding, RE-HASHES it against the recorded `prompt_sha` (mismatch ⇒ surface, never
  spawn), then — durability BEFORE effect — (i) appends a `spawn_intent` event under the
  repo lock, (ii) `herdr tab create` (label `i###-<slug>`), (iii) `herdr agent start`
  (worker name `i###-<slug>-D###A##`, argv from `automation.spawn_argv`, a list whose
  literal `{prompt}` element is replaced by the durable prompt path), (iv) records the
  `DISPATCHED→ACKED` with the created tab id in the SAME tick (this same-tick ACK is the
  ONLY automated ACK — track created that tab itself, so the join is corroborated).
  Alongside the snapshot-based capacity check, the under-lock re-check enforces a
  **durable capacity count** (open `spawn_intent` leases + ACKED automation dispatches on
  the log) so a stale/optimistic snapshot or a concurrent tick can only UNDER-spawn, never
  overshoot `max_live_workers`. A crash mid-sequence leaves the durable intent: the next
  tick's recovery pass re-joins it to live panes by worker name but **NEVER auto-ACKs on
  that name join** (herdr allows duplicate pane names, and the deterministic worker name is
  derivable — a name match is impostor-forgeable). Exactly one matching pane surfaces
  `spawn-ack-unconfirmed` with the candidate pane and the exact
  `bin/record dispatch <D> ACKED --tab <id>` command for the operator; several matches
  surface `ambiguous-spawn-ack`; none — after `automation.spawn_ack_timeout_ticks`
  herdr-visible ticks — surfaces `suspected-orphan-intent`, carrying the last known
  spawn-window failure cause when one was recorded (track NEVER auto-abandons and never
  re-spawns while an intent is open). **A transient herdr error in the spawn window
  (tab-create/agent-start failure) therefore requires manual adjudication to re-queue:**
  abandon the attempt (`FAILED`) by hand and re-prep — track never auto-retries a
  dispatch whose lease is open. ACKED is the ceiling: results, verification, merges, and
  closes keep their existing gates. All spawn anomalies surface in `TRACKER.md`
  (`prompt-sha-mismatch`, `name-collision`, `herdr-tab-create-failed`,
  `herdr-start-failed`, `spawn-ack-unconfirmed`, `ambiguous-spawn-ack`,
  `suspected-orphan-intent`, `spawn-config-invalid`; steady-state counts
  `capacity-exceeded`, `auto-spawn-disabled`).
  **Prompt-integrity residual (documented deliberately):** the recorded `prompt_sha` is
  re-hashed against the durable copy at plan time AND again under the repo lock
  immediately before the intent is appended — but delivery to the worker is **by path**:
  herdr's worker reads `prompts/…` from disk after `agent start`, so the guard binds the
  spawn-time bytes only, not what the file contains when the worker later reads it.
  `prompts/` is git-tracked and treated as operator-trust territory; a write there after
  spawn is an operator action, not something track can or should police.

## 5. herdr workspace + tab naming

- **Workspace:** `{{HERDR_WORKSPACE}}` for all campaign work; the manager tab stays
  `{{CAMPAIGN_SLUG}}-PM`.
- **Tab label = the ticket handle:** `i###-<slug>`, short, lowercase. For pure questions use
  the `Q` number: `q003-<slug>`.
- **One tab ↔ one ticket (or one Q).** Close a ticket's tab when it closes; don't let stale
  idle tabs accumulate.
- **cwd:** start the tab in the primary repo the ticket touches, or a dedicated worktree.

## 6. Model routing (operator-set, not auto-applied)

The model is set by the person launching the worker session BEFORE pasting the prompt — a
`Model:` header line is an instruction to that operator, not something the agent reads.
Defaults by work shape (a *default*, revisit as models change):

{{MODEL_ROUTING}}

When unsure between two tiers, take the cheaper one for execution and the dearer one for
verdicts.

## 7. Branch + worktree naming (all repos)

- Branch: `i###-<slug>` (lowercase ticket + short slug). One ticket per branch.
- Worktree dir: `{{WORKTREE_ROOT}}/<RepoName>-i###-<slug>`.
- Lifecycle: worktree removed + branch deleted once merged into the repo's mainline
  (see the config block). Provenance needs no live branches — manifests/SHAs are re-checkoutable.

## 8. Prompt self-containment — HARD RULES (a dispatched session sees ONLY the pasted text)

A worker session has **no access** to this repo, these conventions, or any `I/Q/R/D-###`
numbering. It sees the pasted prompt and nothing else. Therefore:

1. **The header table is operator-facing** (above the "paste below this line" marker, NOT
   pasted to the agent). Ticket ids, tab names, cross-references belong here.
2. **Everything below the paste marker must be self-contained.** No bare `I-###`/`Q-###`/
   `R-###`/`D-###`, no tab names, no "the PM"/"the ledger"/"the manager tracks" as if the
   agent can resolve them. If a concept from another ticket is needed, **describe the thing**,
   not its number (say "the unmerged fix `<sha>` in worktree X", not "I-005").
3. **The only allowed internal token in the pasted body is the dispatch's OWN id**, and only
   as a report-back label ("cite D-### when you report so it can be filed").
4. **Ground every reference in something the agent can reach:** absolute paths, git SHAs,
   branch/worktree names, file:line, env names, commands. A SHA or path is self-resolving; a
   ticket number is not.
5. **Self-check before dispatch:** re-read only the pasted portion, as if you were the cold
   agent. Every noun must be resolvable from the text or the filesystem. `bin/lint-prompt`
   is a floor for this check — necessary, never sufficient. If any internal label survives
   below the marker, the prompt is not ready.

This rule is load-bearing: a prompt that leaks internal labels produces an agent guessing at
scope — the exact miscommunication this repo exists to prevent.

## 9. Request flow

1. File/update the ticket (`LEDGER.md`) — including its `Verifier:` and `Approval gates:`
   fields (§4) — and append the state event to the log.
2. Write a self-contained prompt file `prompts/<ticket>_<slug>_YYYY-MM-DD.md` with an
   operator-facing header (Ticket, Target repo + worktree, herdr workspace + tab, Model,
   Effort, Deliverable, Report-back format) and a self-contained body (§8). The body MUST
   carry the issue's declared verifier and approval gates — grounded per §8 (commands,
   paths, SHAs; never a ticket number) — and MUST instruct the worker to structure its
   return as **Completed / Verification / Remaining Work**, with Verification answering
   the declared verifier (§4).
3. For persistent work orders, append a pointer to the target repo's `REQUESTS.md`.
4. Dispatch via the chosen lane; record the `dispatch_state` event.
5. Results return through the lane; record the result + verify; update the ticket.

Once a session is **live** (already dispatched, already running in a tab), further
instructions don't go through a new prompt — they go in `messages/`. A reply to the worker's
question, a go-ahead to merge/commit, or a scope change is written to
`messages/<I-###>_<verb>_<slug>_YYYY-MM-DD.md` (verb, e.g. `go`, `reply`) and pasted into the
live tab. The same prompt self-containment rules (§8) apply here: a message pasted into a
live session is just as unable to resolve a bare `I/Q/R/D-###` as a fresh prompt is — ground
it the same way. Both `prompts/` and `messages/` filenames lead with the issue id
`<I-###>_`, so everything for a given issue sorts and greps together.

### Two-channel discipline (per repo, untracked, NEVER committed)
- **`REQUESTS.md`** (repo root) = INBOUND channel to the session that owns that repo's work.
- **`STATUS.md`** (repo root) = that session's REPLY channel; requesters watch it.
- **`PM.md`** (repo root) = a back-pointer stub naming THIS PM repo, so a worker landing in a
  code repo can find where its work is coordinated. One line — the PM repo's path — is enough.

## 10. Manager scope

The manager owns **state + coordination, never PRODUCT code.** It DOES mutate PM state
through the `bin/` tools and its own edits to this repo. It delegates all code execution to
worker sessions.

**Standing duty — backups.** If this PM repo has no offsite remote (see `REMOTE_POLICY` in
CONFIG below), there is no copy of the ledger but the local disk. At every major milestone the
manager reminds the user to take a manual backup.

## 11. Closing a session (archive)

When an issue's work is fully done, `bin/close <I-###>` transitions the issue to CLOSED and
**moves** its `prompts/` and `messages/` files into `archive/prompts/` and
`archive/messages/` respectively — matched by the leading `<I-###>_` filename prefix.
`reports/` is **never** touched by close: it stays in place as the permanent audit trail.
`bin/close` is idempotent (running it again on an already-closed issue is a no-op) and
supports `--dry-run` to preview the move without touching the filesystem.

